<h1>Sanitary Conveyors: Hygienic Design Standards</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Sanitary conveyors are food-processing conveyor systems designed for thorough cleaning through washdown, CIP (Clean-in-Place), or COP (Clean-out-of-Place) methods. They use IP65 through IP69K rated components, stainless steel frames with crevice-free welds, self-draining surfaces, and FDA-compliant contact materials to meet <a href="https://www.ams.usda.gov/sites/default/files/media/Guidelines%20for%20the%20Evaluation%20and%20Certification%20of%20the%20Sanitary%20Design%20and%20Fabrication%20of%20Meat%20and%20Poultry%20Processing%20Equipment.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">USDA acceptance</a> and <a href="https://www.3-a.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">3-A Sanitary Standards</a>.</p>
<p>A sanitary washdown conveyor combines two requirements: components that survive wet cleaning, and hygienic geometry that leaves no place for residue or bacteria to hide. This page covers the hygienic design side. For component ratings, heavy washdown materials, and failure modes, see <a href="/conveyor-types/washdown-conveyors/">washdown conveyors</a>. For spray bars, belt washers, and scrapers, see <a href="/components/cleaning-systems/">conveyor cleaning systems</a>.</p>
<h2 id="ip-heading">IP Ratings in Sanitary Zones: IP65, IP66 &amp; IP69K</h2>
<p>The Ingress Protection (IP) rating system (IEC 60529) uses two digits to describe protection level. The first digit rates solid particle protection (6 = dust-tight). The second digit rates water protection. Food conveyors typically start at IP65 and go up to IP69K depending on the severity of the cleaning protocol.</p>
<p><strong>Choosing the wrong IP rating is a costly specification mistake.</strong> Under-specifying causes premature motor failures, bearing corrosion, and electrical faults. Over-specifying wastes budget that could go toward additional conveyors or automation. The right choice depends entirely on your sanitation protocol.</p>
<h3>IP65: Low-Pressure Water Protection</h3>
<p>IP65 protects against water jets from any direction. The IPX5 test uses a 6.3 mm nozzle delivering 12.5 liters per minute from 3 meters (<a href="https://keystonecompliance.com/ipx5-ipx6-water-jets/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Keystone Compliance</a>). This rating handles splash zones, occasional wipe-down cleaning, and environments where the conveyor gets wet but does not face direct hose-down cleaning.</p>
<p><strong>Use IP65 for:</strong> dry food packaging areas with periodic cleaning, beverage secondary packaging, bakery environments without direct washdown, and enclosed product-handling areas where only incidental splashing occurs.</p>
<h3>IP66: High-Pressure Water Protection</h3>
<p>IP66 protects against powerful water jets from any direction (12.5 mm nozzle, 100 liters per minute from 3 meters). This rating handles standard food plant washdown where sanitation crews use pressure washers and spray wands during shift changes or end-of-day cleaning.</p>
<p><strong>Use IP66 for:</strong> standard food processing washdown environments, produce handling, canning lines, and facilities where daily hose-down cleaning is part of the sanitation SOP.</p>
<h3>IP69K: High-Pressure, High-Temperature Protection</h3>
<p>IP69K, commonly referenced from DIN 40050-9 and ISO 20653 test methods, protects against close-range high-pressure, high-temperature water jets. The test sprays 80 C (176 F) water at 80-100 bar from 10-15 cm, for 30 seconds at each of four angles (0, 30, 60, and 90 degrees) while the device turns on a turntable (<a href="https://keystonecompliance.com/ip69k-ingress-protection-testing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Keystone Compliance</a>).</p>
<p><strong>Use IP69K for:</strong> meat and poultry processing, dairy plants, seafood handling, and any facility running CIP (Clean-in-Place) cycles, high-pressure foam-and-rinse sanitation, or steam cleaning. No USDA rule mandates a specific IP rating, but meat and poultry plants commonly specify IP69K motors and drives in processing areas because of their washdown procedures.</p>
<p>IP69K components typically use stainless steel housings, special shaft seals, and sealed electrical connections. They cost several times more than standard TEFC motors, which is why the rating should match the actual cleaning procedure.</p>
