<h2 id="cip-heading">CIP and COP Cleaning for Food Conveyors</h2>
<p>Food plants use two primary cleaning approaches for conveyor systems: CIP (Clean-in-Place) and COP (Clean-out-of-Place). The cleaning method affects conveyor design, component selection, and total cost of ownership. Most food plants use a combination of both methods on different parts of the same conveyor system.</p>
<h3>CIP (Clean-in-Place)</h3>
<p>CIP cleaning uses automated spray systems to circulate cleaning solution, rinse water, and sanitizer across conveyor surfaces without disassembly. Spray nozzles mount along the conveyor frame and target all product-contact surfaces. A typical CIP cycle runs: pre-rinse, alkaline wash, intermediate rinse, acid wash, final rinse, and sanitizer application.</p>
<p>CIP-capable conveyors require specific design features:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Self-draining frame design:</strong> All surfaces slope toward drain points with no horizontal ledges or pockets that trap liquid.</li>
<li><strong>Smooth, crevice-free welds:</strong> All welds ground flush to eliminate bacterial harborage points. No skip welds or spot welds on product-contact surfaces.</li>
<li><strong>Sealed penetrations:</strong> Every bolt hole, bearing mount, and wire entry uses food-grade gaskets or silicone seals to prevent cleaning solution from entering cavities.</li>
<li><strong>IP69K components:</strong> All motors, drives, sensors, and enclosures withstand the temperature and pressure of CIP cleaning chemistry.</li>
<li><strong>No dead legs:</strong> No tubing or frame cavities where cleaning solution can pool without drainage.</li>
</ul>
<h3>COP (Clean-out-of-Place)</h3>
<p>COP cleaning requires removing components from the conveyor and cleaning them separately. The most common COP components are belts, rollers, and guards. Sanitation crews remove these parts and wash them in a COP tank or automated parts washer, then reinstall them after drying and inspection.</p>
<p>Conveyors designed for COP cleaning feature:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tool-free belt removal:</strong> Quick-release tensioners allow the belt to be removed without wrenches or keys. Dorner's <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/sanitary-stainless-steel-conveyors/aquapruf-conveyors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AquaPruf</a> tip-up tails are one example.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">Modular plastic belts</a>:</strong> Individual belt links snap apart for tank cleaning. Acetal or polypropylene links resist cleaning chemicals and handle repeated disassembly cycles.</li>
<li><strong>Hinged guards and covers:</strong> Guards swing open or lift off without tools for full frame access.</li>
<li><strong>Quick-disconnect rollers:</strong> Rollers pop out of the frame for tank cleaning or replacement without removing the conveyor from the line.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Combined CIP/COP Approach</h3>
<p>Many food plants combine CIP and COP. The conveyor frame and drive components clean via CIP spray systems during the sanitation cycle. The belt removes for COP tank cleaning, where it soaks in sanitizer solution, gets scrubbed, rinsed, and inspected before reinstallation. This combination provides the most thorough sanitation while keeping line downtime manageable.</p>
<p>The spray bars, belt washers, and scrapers that deliver CIP chemistry are covered in <a href="/components/cleaning-systems/">conveyor cleaning systems</a>.</p>
<h2 id="design-heading">Sanitary Design Principles for Food Conveyors</h2>
<p>Sanitary design goes beyond material selection. The geometry of every surface, joint, and cavity determines whether the conveyor can be cleaned effectively. USDA, 3-A, and <a href="https://www.bissc.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BISSC</a> standards all reference the same core design principles, even though each standard applies them differently.</p>
<h3>Surface Finish Requirements</h3>
<p>3-A Sanitary Standards call for product-contact surfaces, including welded joints, to be at least as smooth as 32 microinch (0.8 micrometer) Ra and free of pits, folds, cracks, and crevices (<a href="https://my.3-a.org/Portals/93/Documents/Annual%20Meetings%20Presentations/May1_Basics_02_Hygienic%20Design%20Considerations%20and%20Techniques.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">3-A SSI hygienic design presentation</a>). Non-contact surfaces should be smooth enough to prevent soil accumulation. Electropolishing provides a smoother, more corrosion-resistant surface than mechanical polishing because it removes the surface layer of disturbed metal left by grinding operations.</p>
<h3>Corner Radii and Crevice Elimination</h3>
<p>All internal corners on product-contact surfaces require minimum radii to prevent product accumulation and bacterial growth. 3-A criteria call for radii of at least 1/4 inch (about 6 mm) on internal angles less than 135 degrees, with exceptions for gasket and O-ring grooves. Larger radii are easier to clean. Square corners trap product and resist cleaning, so bacteria can establish in these dead zones and contaminate later production runs.</p>
<p>Sanitary conveyor manufacturers eliminate crevices by using continuous welds (no skip welds), avoiding threaded fasteners on product-contact surfaces, and using formed one-piece components instead of assembled multi-piece brackets.</p>
<h3>Frame Design: Open vs. Enclosed</h3>
<p>Sanitary food conveyors use open-frame construction that exposes all surfaces for inspection and cleaning. Hollow tubes and closed box sections trap moisture, cleaning chemicals, and bacteria inside cavities that cannot be accessed. Open-profile frames or solid round-tube frames with continuous welds and no internal cavities are the standard sanitary approach.</p>
<p>The frame must also allow cleaning access underneath the conveyor. Conveyors mounted on cantilever supports or open legs allow sanitation crews to clean the floor and underside without blind spots. Conveyors bolted to solid pads or enclosed bases create harborage zones under the equipment.</p>
<h3>Self-Draining Surfaces</h3>
<p>All frame surfaces must slope toward drain points. Standing water creates bacterial growth environments and dilutes sanitizer concentration. Self-draining design is especially critical on conveyor side rails, supports, and the underside of the belt return path.</p>
<h3>Bearings, Seals, and Lubrication</h3>
<p>Bearings in sanitary conveyors use sealed housings that prevent cleaning water from entering the bearing cavity and prevent lubricant from leaking onto the product. Bearings where incidental food contact is possible use lubricants that meet <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/178.3570" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 178.3570</a>, typically sold as NSF H1 registered. Sealed stainless steel bearing housings resist corrosion from cleaning chemicals.</p>
<p>Some sanitary conveyors use self-lubricating polymer bearings that eliminate lubricant contamination risk entirely. These bearings cost more and wear faster than steel bearings but eliminate the need for lubricant management in food contact zones.</p>
