<p>Modular plastic ranges are from Intralox's <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-900/perforated-flat-top" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Series 900 Perforated Flat Top</a> listing. Actual limits vary by manufacturer, belt construction, and chemical exposure, so confirm them on the supplier's data sheet.</p>
<h3>Metal-Detectable Belting</h3>
<p>Metal-detectable belt materials contain additives that allow inline metal detectors to identify belt fragments if the belt wears or breaks. Standard colors are blue (for visual detection against most food products) or white. Plants often specify metal-detectable belting as part of the foreign-material controls they document for SQF, BRCGS, or FSSC 22000 audits.</p>
<h3>Belt Color Standards</h3>
<p>White and blue are the standard belt colors for food conveyors. White provides the best visual contrast for most contaminants. Blue is visible against nearly all food products and makes foreign-body detection easier during visual inspection. Some food plants use different colors for different product zones (e.g., red for raw meat, blue for cooked product) to prevent cross-contamination through belt mix-ups during COP cleaning.</p>
<h2 id="acceptance-heading">USDA and 3-A Acceptance for Sanitary Conveyors</h2>
<p>USDA acceptance and 3-A authorization are separate processes with different requirements, different governing bodies, and different scopes of application. Understanding which applies to your facility prevents over-specifying (wasting budget) or under-specifying (failing audit).</p>
<h3>USDA Acceptance Process</h3>
<p>The USDA AMS Dairy Grading Branch runs the meat and poultry equipment review. Manufacturers submit engineering drawings showing dimensions, materials, internal-angle radii, product-contact surface finish, and weld specifications. Reviewers then inspect completed units for workmanship and adherence to the accepted drawings. Plastic and rubber product-contact materials need certification that they comply with FDA regulations in 21 CFR Parts 170-199. Equipment that passes can receive an Equipment Acceptance Certificate for the model or model series and is listed in the Accepted Meat and Poultry Equipment Listing. Acceptance must be re-validated every four years, and design or material changes must be submitted for review (<a href="https://www.ams.usda.gov/sites/default/files/media/Guidelines%20for%20the%20Evaluation%20and%20Certification%20of%20the%20Sanitary%20Design%20and%20Fabrication%20of%20Meat%20and%20Poultry%20Processing%20Equipment.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">USDA AMS guidelines</a>).</p>
<p>The review is voluntary. FSIS ended mandatory prior approval of plant equipment in 1997, and AMS states it does not require equipment to be presented for review. FSIS inspectors can still ask a plant to modify equipment that appears to violate the NSF/ANSI/3-A 14159 standards, so AMS encourages buyers to make USDA acceptance a purchase contract provision.</p>
<h3>3-A Sanitary Standards Authorization</h3>
<p>3-A authorization requires third-party verification that equipment meets the applicable 3-A standard.<!-- VERIFY: 3-A Symbol Third Party Verification (TPV) process and evaluator title on 3-a.org --> An authorized 3-A evaluator inspects the equipment against published criteria for material composition, surface finish, cleanability, drainability, and design geometry. Equipment meeting all criteria earns the right to display the 3-A symbol.</p>
<p>3-A standards apply primarily to dairy processing equipment. Many processors also use 3-A design principles as a benchmark for other food-contact equipment. Meeting 3-A criteria provides a defensible standard of sanitary design during audits, even for non-dairy applications.</p>
