<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Specify a Sanitary Conveyor</h2>
<p>Specifying a sanitary conveyor correctly requires answering these questions before contacting manufacturers. Missing any one of these leads to respecification, change orders, or (worst case) a conveyor that fails audit.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>What is the product?</strong> Raw or cooked? Direct contact or sealed package? Temperature at the conveyor (frozen, ambient, hot)?</li>
<li><strong>What is the cleaning protocol?</strong> Wipe-down, hose-down, high-pressure washdown, CIP, or COP? Frequency (every shift, daily, weekly)?</li>
<li><strong>What cleaning chemicals are used?</strong> Quaternary ammonium, chlorinated alkaline, peracetic acid, caustic, or other? This determines 304 vs. 316 stainless and belt material compatibility.</li>
<li><strong>Which regulatory standard applies?</strong> USDA, 3-A, BISSC, or general <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/food-ingredients-packaging/packaging-food-contact-substances-fcs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDA</a>/<a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-preventive-controls-human-food" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FSMA</a> compliance? FDA's CGMP rule (<a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/117.40" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 117.40</a>) requires cleanable equipment and corrosion-resistant food-contact surfaces in every registered food facility.</li>
<li><strong>What is the environment?</strong> Ambient dry, humid, wet floor, outdoor, or temperature-controlled?</li>
<li><strong>What throughput is required?</strong> Products per minute, line speed (FPM), and <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation requirements</a>.</li>
<li><strong>What upstream/downstream equipment connects?</strong> <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">Transfer points</a>, elevation changes, and <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">laning/collating</a> requirements.</li>
</ol>
<p>Collect these answers into your RFQ document before requesting quotes. This ensures you receive comparable quotes from multiple manufacturers and prevents scope creep during the project.</p>
