<li><a href="https://my.3-a.org/Portals/93/Documents/Annual%20Meetings%20Presentations/May1_Basics_02_Hygienic%20Design%20Considerations%20and%20Techniques.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hygienic Design Standards and Guidelines</a>, L. Hanson, 3-A SSI. 32 µin (0.8 µm) Ra product-contact finish, 1/4 inch radii on angles under 135 degrees.</li>
<li><a href="https://keystonecompliance.com/ipx5-ipx6-water-jets/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IPX5 and IPX6 Water Jets Testing</a>, Keystone Compliance. Nozzle size, flow rate, and distance.</li>
<li><a href="https://keystonecompliance.com/ip69k-ingress-protection-testing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IP69K Ingress Protection Testing</a>, Keystone Compliance. 80 C, 80-100 bar, 10-15 cm, four spray angles.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/117.40" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 117.40, Equipment and utensils</a>, U.S. FDA (via Cornell LII). Cleanable equipment, corrosion-resistant food-contact surfaces.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/178.3570" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 178.3570, Lubricants with incidental food contact</a>, U.S. FDA (via Cornell LII). Food-grade lubricant basis.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-preventive-controls-human-food" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FSMA Final Rule for Preventive Controls for Human Food</a>, U.S. FDA. Hazard analysis, preventive controls, and updated CGMPs.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-900/perforated-flat-top" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Series 900 Perforated Flat Top</a>, Intralox. Polypropylene and acetal temperature ranges.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/sanitary-stainless-steel-conveyors/aquapruf-conveyors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AquaPruf Conveyors</a>, Dorner. Tip-up tails, tool-less disassembly.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is the difference between IP65, IP66, and IP69K?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><strong>IP65</strong> handles water jets (6.3 mm nozzle, 12.5 L/min), suitable for splash zones and light cleaning. <strong>IP66</strong> handles powerful water jets (12.5 mm nozzle, 100 L/min), suitable for routine hose-down. <strong>IP69K</strong> handles close-range high-pressure, high-temperature water (80 C / 176 F at 80-100 bar), the usual choice where sanitation uses high-pressure hot water or foam-and-rinse washdown.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is CIP cleaning on a food conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><strong>CIP (Clean-in-Place)</strong> uses automated spray systems to clean the conveyor without disassembly. Spray nozzles mounted along the conveyor circulate cleaning solution, rinse water, and sanitizer across all product-contact surfaces. CIP-capable conveyors need self-draining frames, smooth crevice-free welds, sealed penetrations, and washdown-rated (often IP69K) components.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between CIP and COP for conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><strong>CIP (Clean-in-Place)</strong> cleans the conveyor without disassembly using automated spray systems. <strong>COP (Clean-out-of-Place)</strong> requires removing parts (belts, rollers, guards) and cleaning them in a separate wash station or tank. Many food conveyors use a combination: the frame and drive components clean via CIP, while the belt removes for COP cleaning in a sanitizing tank. Modular plastic belts support COP because individual links snap out for tank cleaning.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">Do I need USDA acceptance for my food conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><strong>Not by law.</strong> FSIS ended mandatory prior approval of meat and poultry plant equipment in 1997. USDA AMS now runs a voluntary review against the NSF/ANSI/3-A 14159 standards, including 14159-3 for mechanical belt conveyors. AMS does not require equipment to be presented for review, but FSIS inspectors can ask a plant to modify equipment that appears to violate the standards.</p>
<p>Many buyers write USDA acceptance into the purchase contract for exposed-product conveyors to reduce that risk. Conveyors that only carry sealed packages usually do not need it.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">What surface finish do sanitary conveyors require?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>3-A Sanitary Standards call for product-contact surfaces, including welds, at least as smooth as <strong>32 microinch (0.8 micrometer) Ra</strong>. Electropolishing can produce a smoother, more corrosion-resistant finish than mechanical polishing. Non-contact surfaces should be smooth enough to prevent soil accumulation and facilitate cleaning.</p>

</div>
</div>
